","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/VeloBits/velobits-UI/commit/edb84e9c09693573496a32ddaf7ce07ff88e6191","node_id":"C_kwDOTuZyj9oAKGVkYjg0ZTljMDk2OTM1NzM0OTZhMzJkZGFmN2NlMDdmZjg4ZTYxOTE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/VeloBits/velobits-UI/commit/08b19c9a005bc6e8e4c38c194cda8319c797b5a8","sha":"08b19c9a005bc6e8e4c38c194cda8319c797b5a8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/VeloBits/velobits-UI/commits/08b19c9a005bc6e8e4c38c194cda8319c797b5a8"},{"html_url":"https://github.com/VeloBits/velobits-UI/commit/b0484524c7f1679b40f2f8d872a0829b961f7918","sha":"b0484524c7f1679b40f2f8d872a0829b961f7918","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/VeloBits/velobits-UI/commits/b0484524c7f1679b40f2f8d872a0829b961f7918"}],"sha":"edb84e9c09693573496a32ddaf7ce07ff88e6191","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/VeloBits/velobits-UI/commits/edb84e9c09693573496a32ddaf7ce07ff88e6191"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/50881320?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/itsSauraj/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/itsSauraj/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/itsSauraj/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/itsSauraj/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/itsSauraj","id":50881320,"login":"itsSauraj","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwODgxMzIw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/itsSauraj/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/itsSauraj/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/itsSauraj/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/itsSauraj/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/itsSauraj/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/itsSauraj","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/VeloBits/velobits-UI/commits/b0484524c7f1679b40f2f8d872a0829b961f7918/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-14T12:33:20Z","email":"sauraj.contact@gmail.com","name":"Saurabh Yadav"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-14T12:33:20Z","email":"sauraj.contact@gmail.com","name":"Saurabh Yadav"},"message":"fix(release): tell \"scope does not exist\" apart from \"members are private\"\n\nThe preflight read the scope''s member list as\n`curl -fsSL … || echo ''{}''`, which collapsed two unrelated answers into one.\n`-f` makes curl fail on 404, so an unregistered scope produced the same empty\nmap a real org returns, and the unambiguous case — the name does not exist, so\nnothing can ever publish into it — surfaced as the soft warning meant for\n\"members are private\". That is exactly backwards: the knowable failure was the\none being guessed at.\n\nStatus and body are now read separately, and each answer means one thing:\n\n  404          the name is unregistered            -> hard fail\n  200 {\"u\":…}  a USER scope, membership is public  -> decide here\n  200 {}       an ORG, members are private         -> ask npm as ourselves\n\nThe last case is new and is the one that matters. An unauthenticated read cannot\nsee org members, so the old code could only shrug at the single most likely\ncause of a refused publish: the account is simply not in the org. `npm org ls`\nrun with our own token answers it, and distinguishes \"not a member\" from\n\"member, insufficient role to enumerate\" — the latter stays a warning, because\nfailing it would block legitimate releases.\n\nThe warning text on that path now also names the case it cannot rule out: a\ntoken issued against a DIFFERENT org looks identical from outside. That is worth\nsaying, because renaming an npm org leaves a granular token still passing\n`npm whoami` while no longer matching the scope it must write to, which fails as\nthe same masked 404 and reads as though nothing changed.\n\nVerified against the live registry — all four branches resolve correctly:\nvelobitsio 200 {} -> org path; velobits 200 {\"velobits\":\"owner\"} -> hard fail as\nanother account''s personal scope; an unregistered name 404 -> hard fail; and\nvelobits-team 200 -> owns-scope pass. YAML and bash both parse; format and lint\nclean.\n\nThis changes diagnosis only. It publishes nothing that would not have published\nbefore, and fixes no permission by itself.","tree":{"sha":"f8f2e124663cd52c546b28880066de776cbe5b7d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/VeloBits/velobits-UI/git/trees/f8f2e124663cd52c546b28880066de776cbe5b7d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/VeloBits/velobits-UI/git/commits/b0484524c7f1679b40f2f8d872a0829b961f7918","verification":{"payload":"tree f8f2e124663cd52c546b28880066de776cbe5b7d\nparent 08b19c9a005bc6e8e4c38c194cda8319c797b5a8\nauthor Saurabh Yadav <sauraj.contact@gmail.com> 1786710800 +0530\ncommitter Saurabh Yadav <sauraj.contact@gmail.com> 1786710800 +0530\n\nfix(release): tell \"scope does not exist\" apart from \"members are private\"\n\nThe preflight read the scope''s member list as\n`curl -fsSL … || echo ''{}''`, which collapsed two unrelated answers into one.\n`-f` makes curl fail on 404, so an unregistered scope produced the same empty\nmap a real org returns, and the unambiguous case — the name does not exist, so\nnothing can ever publish into it — surfaced as the soft warning meant for\n\"members are private\". That is exactly backwards: the knowable failure was the\none being guessed at.\n\nStatus and body are now read separately, and each answer means one thing:\n\n  404          the name is unregistered            -> hard fail\n  200 {\"u\":…}  a USER scope, membership is public  -> decide here\n  200 {}       an ORG, members are private         -> ask npm as ourselves\n\nThe last case is new and is the one that matters. An unauthenticated read cannot\nsee org members, so the old code could only shrug at the single most likely\ncause of a refused publish: the account is simply not in the org. `npm org ls`\nrun with our own token answers it, and distinguishes \"not a member\" from\n\"member, insufficient role to enumerate\" — the latter stays a warning, because\nfailing it would block legitimate releases.\n\nThe warning text on that path now also names the case it cannot rule out: a\ntoken issued against a DIFFERENT org looks identical from outside. That is worth\nsaying, because renaming an npm org leaves a granular token still passing\n`npm whoami` while no longer matching the scope it must write to, which fails as\nthe same masked 404 and reads as though nothing changed.\n\nVerified against the live registry — all four branches resolve correctly:\nvelobitsio 200 {} -> org path; velobits 200 {\"velobits\":\"owner\"} -> hard fail as\nanother account''s personal scope; an unregistered name 404 -> hard fail; and\nvelobits-team 200 -> owns-scope pass. YAML and bash both parse; format and lint\nclean.\n\nThis changes diagnosis only. It publishes nothing that would not have published\nbefore, and fixes no permission by itself.\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niQHPBAABCAA5FiEEmTLsOhbjX1ndSoeT9vAKBAaqrIkFAmp/CxAbFIAAAAAABAAO\nbWFudTIsMi41KzEuMTIsMiwxAAoJEPbwCgQGqqyJg