://api.github.com/users/aafaq01","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aafaq01/obsly/commits/ad63ffb1c9a741137ce3dbaf50430357aca547a7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-11T21:08:55Z","email":"m.aafaq.rana@gmail.com","name":"Aafaq Rana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-11T21:08:55Z","email":"m.aafaq.rana@gmail.com","name":"Aafaq Rana"},"message":"feat: alert rules with webhook delivery\n\nEverything in Obsly answered questions when you opened it. Nothing told anyone\nanything — a dashboard nobody has open is not monitoring, and this was the\nlargest remaining gap in the product.\n\nThree triggers, deliberately. Each answers a question somebody actually asks at\n3am, and a rule language whose behaviour nobody can predict is worse than no\nrules at all:\n\n- a new issue appears — the bug nobody has triaged\n- a resolved issue happens again — the case a \"new issue\" rule silently misses,\n  because the issue is not new\n- an issue crosses a rate — the bug that was tolerable at 5 an hour and is not\n  at 500\n\nFrequency counts events inside the window rather than reading times_seen. The\ncounter is lifetime, so an issue that was busy last year and quiet since would\notherwise trip a \"50 in 5 minutes\" rule on its first event today.\n\nCooldowns are per (rule, issue), not per rule. Without one, a bug seen a\nthousand times an hour sends a thousand notifications and the channel gets\nmuted — which is the same as no alerting, with more noise. Per rule would be\nworse than useless: one noisy issue would silence the alert for a different\nissue starting five minutes later.\n\nOne delivery mechanism. Slack, Discord, Teams, PagerDuty and Opsgenie all\naccept an incoming webhook; five bespoke integrations would carry five\ncredential stores and five ways to break.\n\nTwo constraints shaped the delivery path:\n\nIngest must never block on somebody else''s HTTP endpoint. A webhook host that\ntakes thirty seconds would hold an ingest worker for thirty seconds, and an\nalerting feature that takes the system down is worse than none. The POST runs\non a background thread.\n\nA failed delivery must not look like a quiet system. The fire row is written\nbefore delivery is attempted and updated with the outcome, so \"the webhook was\ndown\" and \"nothing happened\" are distinguishable — which is the whole point of\nthe feed sitting on the same page as the rules.\n\nRules can be tested from the UI against a real issue. A webhook you cannot test\nis one you find out is broken during the incident it was supposed to warn you\nabout.\n\nVerified end to end against a live receiver: payload delivered and read back,\nthe cooldown suppressed the duplicate, and an unreachable host was recorded as\nfailed rather than disappearing.\n\nAlso folds the frontend''s patch() and post() into one send(). They were copies\nthat had drifted — one surfaced the server''s message and the other flattened it\ninto a status code — and a third copy for DELETE would have made it worse. The\nsign-in request keeps its own behaviour explicitly: a 401 there means these\ncredentials are wrong, not that a session expired.\n\nFixes five CSS custom properties that were used but never defined, two of which\nshipped in the chart captions last change and were silently inheriting whatever\ncolour surrounded them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"165067cac554f690e9fef08546ec799e363fc537","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aafaq01/obsly/git/trees/165067cac554f690e9fef08546ec799e363fc537"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/aafaq01/obsly/git/commits/ad63ffb1c9a741137ce3dbaf50430357aca547a7","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/33451925?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aafaq01/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/aa